


MKERRLST(1)	    UNIX Programmer's Manual	      MKERRLST(1)



NNAAMMEE
     mkerrlst - create system error file

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmkkeerrrrllsstt [ --ii _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e ] [ --oo _o_u_t_p_u_t_f_i_l_e ]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     MMkkeerrrrllsstt(1) creates error message files in the format
     described by ssyysseerrrrllsstt(5).

     With no arguments mmkkeerrrrllsstt creates the file /_e_t_c/_s_y_s_e_r_r_l_s_t
     from the internal array ssyyss__eerrrrlliisstt.

     Give just the --oo option mmkkeerrrrllsstt will create the file _o_u_t_-
     _p_u_t_f_i_l_e from the internal array ssyyss__eerrrrlliisstt.

     Given just the --ii option mmkkeerrrrllsstt will create the file
     /_e_t_c/_s_y_s_e_r_r_l_s_t from the input file _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e.

     Given both --ii and --oo options mmkkeerrrrllsstt will create the error
     message file _o_u_t_p_u_t_f_i_l_e from the strings contained in _i_n_p_u_t_-
     _f_i_l_e.

     NNOOTTEE:: error messages are numbered from 0.	If the error 0
     does not have a message associated with it the first string
     in _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e must still be present.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
     mmkkeerrrrllsstt exits with status of 0 if no errors are encoun-
     tered.  If errors do occur an error message is printed on
     _s_t_d_e_r_r and the exit status is 1.

EERRRROORRSS
     mmkkeerrrrllsstt(1) can encounter any of the errors for the ooppeenn(2),
     llsseeeekk(2), rreeaadd(2), or wwrriittee((22)) system calls.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     syserrlst(3) syserrlst(5)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     mmkkeerrrrllsstt(1), first appeared in 2.11BSD.

BBUUGGSS
     Error messages can be a maximum of 80 characters.











